1
00:00:01.967 --> 00:00:05.000
Very exciting topic now. We're going to talk about CrewAI flows and agents.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.000
And this is important because when you go into the wild, the actual use cases,

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.000
usually they live in the spectrum. And we're going to talk about that in a second.

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.000
But this spectrum goes from less agency, where you want more control,

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.000
into more agency, where you want the agents to figure out as they go.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.000
But the important thing about flows here is that it's a very thin,

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:33.000
low-level layer that gives you all the control that you need to decide what happens in what order.

8
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.000
And then you can opt in how much agency you want.

9
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.000
This is a very exciting topic, and it's going to be a huge unlock for many use cases,

10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:46.000
especially the use cases that require more precision. So let's look into that right now.

11
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.000
So up to this point, we have been talking a lot about agents themselves,

12
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.000
but there are other mental models for agentic systems.

13
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.000
Generally, AI agents are understood through three main mental models,

14
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:05.000
graphs, events, and agents. And up to this point, agents have been very exciting for us,

15
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.000
and we have seen how powerful they can be.

16
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Another mental model for agentic systems that has been picking up is this idea of graphs,

17
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.000
where they work with more traditional graph architectures, like nodes and edges.

18
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.000
But the adoption has been a little slower, and friction has been a little higher,

19
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.000
since there are many concepts and graphs that are harder to grasp,

20
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.000
and not many people get exposed to those on a day-to-day basis.

21
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.000
And then there is the events mental model that is gaining a lot of popularity,

22
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.000
because it resembles more familiar PubSub patterns common on the web,

23
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
like most people have been developing web apps up to this point.

24
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.000
So it makes it a little easier for most people to grasp these concepts

25
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.000
and get up and running and productive with that.

26
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Independently of what the mental model that you are using, they all share some patterns

27
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.000
and they all share some common ground around using the LLMs and deciding when to use the LLMs,

28
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.000
and trying to be very smart about what kind of context you are adding into them.

29
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.000
Given these trends, most people, and CrewAI in particular, are focusing on the events

30
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.000
and the agents model when implementing agentic systems.

31
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.000
And we have talked a lot about agents and how you can view them and crews now,

32
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.000
and that has been very exciting.

33
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.000
I wanted to move into focusing a little more on the events-based structure,

34
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:26.000
and how that mental model applies, and how you can use that not only stand-alone,

35
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.000
but with agents and with crews, and how powerful that can be.

36
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.000
We're seeing now millions of executions of flows a day

37
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.000
by using these combinations of CrewAI flows with agents.

38
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.000
So let's dive into that.

39
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Flows are a very modular orchestration layer.

40
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Think about it as a backbone of what you want your agents and your crews to be.

41
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.000
And what that means is that it allows you to define on a very low level

42
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.000
everything that you want to happen in what order.

43
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.000
And there's a few different pieces that you can bring into your flows.

44
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:03.000
You can bring LLMs, you can bring regular Python code,

45
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.000
you can bring one single agent using memory and guardrails, for example,

46
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.000
or you can bring an entire crew.

47
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.000
So in the end of the day, you can bring the entire set of things

48
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.000
that we learned up to this point inside the flow,

49
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.000
and the flow will provide you with the backbone and the structure

50
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.000
to define what is going to be executed in what order.

51
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.000
You can see how powerful that can be,

52
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.000
especially when you want to control a lot of these flows as they get more complex.

53
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:35.000
So a good example of a flow could be something like this.

54
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.000
You might have initial code that is being executed,

55
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.000
And this code might pull data from somewhere or check a specific behavior.

56
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.000
This is just regular Python.

57
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.000
But then from that code, you're going to have it to do two things in parallel.

58
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:52.000
You might want to have that information to flow into a single LLM call.

59
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.000
And this LLM can be doing anything.

60
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.000
It can be filtering out information or extracting context

61
00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.000
or making decisions whether it has all the information it needs or not.

62
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:04.000
And meanwhile, we can also have an agent working alone.

63
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.000
And this single agent will do something with that information.

64
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.000
They might have a memory, they might have guardrails,

65
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.000
they might be producing some sort of output that you want.

66
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:18.000
But from that parallel execution, you can then go back into one single function,

67
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.000
and that's where you're going to have more code happening.

68
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.000
And this code will do something with that information,

69
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.000
and then it might trigger two different things.

70
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:32.000
Maybe it logs something into a specific database so that you can monitor that later

71
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:35.000
and kicks off an entire crew as a next step.

72
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:42.000
So you can see here how through code and LLM and agents and crews,

73
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.000
it gives you a lot of control.

74
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:50.000
In this example, you can see how flows become this automated control layer

75
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:55.000
and the backbone that is defining what is happening throughout the execution.

76
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:58.000
Again, it's a very low-level orchestration framework

77
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.000
that gives you the complete control of what is going to happen

78
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:06.000
and allows you to decide how much agency you want to bring into our automation.

79
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.000
Throughout this execution, you not only control the order,

80
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.000
but you also have a state that is shared among the entire flow.

81
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:17.000
And that means that throughout the different code blocks

82
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:20.000
and throughout the different functions and LLM calls,

83
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:25.000
you can actually put things into that state that you're going to be reusing later on.

84
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:30.000
So as this event is happening, you can choose to use this state appropriately

85
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.000
to make sure that you get all the information that you need where it needs to be.

86
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.000
So at the end of the day, if we take one step back,

87
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:43.000
what we're seeing is there are two different complementing ways to build agentic systems.

88
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:48.000
One that is more optimized for agency, where you have agents, tools, and tasks,

89
00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.000
and memory, and everything that we have seen up to this point.

90
00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.000
And you're optimizing for these agents to just figure it out as they go.

91
00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:01.000
And on the other one, you're seeing flows that provides you this backbone of structure

92
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:06.000
where you can still bring agents, LLMs, and entire crews into,

93
00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.000
but now you've got way more control on how you set it up.

94
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.000
So they each have their situations where they shine.

95
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:17.000
Crews who have a lot of more collaborative agents, so they are more autonomous and dynamic,

96
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.000
they're better for exploratory and less deterministic tasks.

97
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:26.000
So if you want to write a screenplay, or create a marketing strategy, or ideate for a landing page,

98
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.000
crews are great at that.

99
00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.000
On the other hand, flows are more low-level, event-driven control layer.

100
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.000
And they are really ideal for structured, repeatable steps with a tight control,

101
00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.000
so you might have auto-respond to an email, for example,

102
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.000
or triggering and receiving an email that you might want to run some code blocks,

103
00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.000
and even a crew to respond to that email, depending on what is on it or not.

104
00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.000
Or it might be great as a meeting assistant,

105
00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:55.000
where the meeting assistant might be triggered when you save a meeting notes doc,

106
00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.000
and then from there generate tasks for the transcript using a crew,

107
00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:04.000
and those tasks then go into Trello or being sent into Slack just using traditional code.

108
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:09.000
So again, the sky is the limit when you're talking about how you can tangle these two together

109
00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.000
to create very complex use cases.

110
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:17.000
A growing pattern among both enterprise and individual engineers that we have been seeing

111
00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:21.000
is this idea of opt-in agency model,

112
00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:26.000
also what I like to call the minimum that I can get away with approach.

113
00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:31.000
It follows the long-standing engineering principle of keep things simple.

114
00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:36.000
This idea is to start with a structured backbone, such as CrewAIFlows,

115
00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:42.000
and then add agency only where and when it's needed at each step.

116
00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.000
So if a code solves a particular problem on its own,

117
00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.000
you don't need to introduce the cost and the latency and even the potential complications

118
00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.000
of an LLM or even an agent.

119
00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:58.000
So you should reserve agents and crews for the complex tasks that they are meant for

120
00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:00.000
where they can actually add value.

121
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.000
And only then, if you do need an LLM, you can start with just one single LLM call

122
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:09.000
and eventually get into an agent or an entire crew.

123
00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:15.000
So there's a lot of different ways where you can choose how much agency you bring into your flow

124
00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:20.000
and that allows you to make sure that you're being very effective and efficient

125
00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.000
on what you're building.

126
00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:24.000
So let's take a look at how we structure a real-life flow.

127
00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.000
In this flow, you have a start conversation function.

128
00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:32.000
And once that conversation starts, it will trigger a router function.

129
00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.000
And that router function is basically doing one single LLM request

130
00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.000
to decide whether it does a deep research or not.

131
00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:42.000
Let's say that this conversation has been going on for a while.

132
00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:47.000
And due to that, the agent and the LLM has already a lot of context.

133
00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:50.000
So there's a lot of messages in the state of this flow.

134
00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.000
Meaning that it doesn't necessarily need to trigger a new research

135
00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:57.000
because it already has all the answers in its context.

136
00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.000
So it can just go straight into an answer function.

137
00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.000
Then we do another LLM call and get back to you with an answer.

138
00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:08.000
Now, what if you have to trigger an entire deep research?

139
00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:13.000
Well, then that goes into a research function that will have an entire crew into that.

140
00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:17.000
And that crew will basically behave the same way that we have seen up to this point.

141
00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:21.000
Where it's going to go out and actually do the research and scrape pages

142
00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:26.000
and put together a final report that will later be used to give you back your answer.

143
00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:30.000
So if you really look into flows, it has a set of building blocks

144
00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:33.000
that allows you to build this very complex use case.

145
00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.000
And they're very simple building blocks because this is just a very thin layer.

146
00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:40.000
And we're going to talk about the three major building blocks here.

147
00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:43.000
The start, the listen, and the router.

148
00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:48.000
And all of those are just decorators that you can annotate your functions with.

149
00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:51.000
So the first one is the start decorator.

150
00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:55.000
And that just marks what is going to be the first function that is going to be executed.

151
00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:58.000
The other one is the listen decorator.

152
00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:04.000
And that basically marks the function that is going to be executed once another function is done.

153
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:10.000
And then you also have the router decorator that defines this is going to be a conditional function.

154
00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:17.000
Where it's going to route requests in different ways depending on what happens inside the function itself.

155
00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:23.000
With these three very simple building blocks alone, there is so much you can do.

156
00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:28.000
And as we can put more blocks together, and we're going to talk about them in a second,

157
00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:30.000
you will see how you can do so much more.

158
00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.000
So here's an example of a very simple flow that leverages all these building blocks.

159
00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:40.000
All you got to do is import those decorators from early on.

160
00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:43.000
So in this case, we're just importing listen and start.

161
00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:46.000
And you can see that this is regular Python.

162
00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:49.000
So it's a very Pythonic way to do things.

163
00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:55.000
Where you're just writing down your functions and you're using these decorators to annotate your functions.

164
00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:02.000
So we're using the start decorator here to flag the first function as being the starting point of the flow.

165
00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:09.000
And then you also have the listen decorator that is basically listening to the start conversation function.

166
00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:17.000
And once that function is done, then it will call the trigger research function that we're annotating with the listen decorator.

167
00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:27.000
So you can see here how simple it is for you to just use start and listen to actually set up all the set of chain of events that you want to happen.

168
00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:30.000
And there's so much more than you can do beyond this basic flow.

169
00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:38.000
So if you look at some examples here real quick, you can see how the combination of these patterns can build something truly interesting.

170
00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:45.000
So you can have a first function that is using the start decorator that then triggers two other functions.

171
00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:48.000
They're listening to the same first function.

172
00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:55.000
So in that way, you can have the second and the third function run in parallel by just using these decorators.

173
00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.000
And then the rest of your flow just keeps going on.

174
00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:01.000
But there's a lot of more patterns in there as well.

175
00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:10.000
For example, you could make sure that you didn't have a for function that only gets executed when the second and the third function finishes.

176
00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:14.000
So when both are done, then that for function will be executed.

177
00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:20.000
So in here, we're using a combination of the listen decorator with the end annotation as well.

178
00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:22.000
And you see how we can do that in code.

179
00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:24.000
It's extremely simple.

180
00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.000
But you don't need to end there.

181
00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:35.000
You can actually have the same for function now set up to be executed either when the second or the third function is done.

182
00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:38.000
So it's another layer on how you can actually set it up.

183
00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:40.000
And there's even more ways.

184
00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:50.000
For example, if we start to use the router decorator, now we can have this second function to trigger either a third one or a fourth one.

185
00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:55.000
So look at all these different combinations and all these abstractions.

186
00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.000
I know they can be a little hard to visualize.

187
00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:04.000
But when you reply them to a real use case, they add so much clarity and they allow you to do so much more.

188
00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:13.000
So let's go back into that example that we talked about before of the conversation with a crew, the deep research use case for flows.

189
00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:15.000
And I want to go over this real quick.

190
00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:25.000
So the starting conversation function would be using the start decorator in this case, indicating that this is where the whole flow starts.

191
00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:35.000
The trigger deep research would be the router decorator that is deciding on whether it's going to send a request in one way or another, depending on what happens inside that function.

192
00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:37.000
And here we're doing an LLM call.

193
00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:43.000
We could be using a function call, for example, from the LLM to decide whether we go one route or the other.

194
00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:49.000
Now, if we go one route for the answer, well, that's going to be listening to a specific one side of the router.

195
00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:53.000
Or if we go with the research, that's going to be listening to another side of the router.

196
00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:55.000
And we're going to see how to do that in a second.

197
00:13:55.000 --> 00:14:00.000
But the cool thing is that as you build these things and as you build this entire workflow,

198
00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:08.000
CrewAI allows you to automatically plot this out so you can actually have a visual cue.

199
00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:13.000
You can have the entire visual system mapped out for you based on your code.

200
00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:19.000
So that makes documentation so much easier and allows you to understand everything that is happening inside your flow.

201
00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:26.000
All you got to do is go into the CrewAI CLI and type CrewAI flow plot.

202
00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:33.000
And what that will do is that will automatically look at all your functions and all your decorators and annotation patterns.

203
00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:35.000
It's going to plot that out for you.

204
00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:42.000
And that allows you, again, to automatically generate these diagrams to help you not only understanding what is happening,

205
00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:45.000
but also make sure that you can document this.

206
00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:50.000
Crews and flows can get very complex, especially when you're just staring at the code all day.

207
00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:55.000
So this really helps you see what is going on and spotting issues with your process.

208
00:14:55.000 --> 00:15:01.000
So here is an example of a deep research hack, but actually mapped out by the CrewAI flow.

209
00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:06.000
You can see here the getUserMessage as the initial starting function,

210
00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:12.000
then going into a router function that will either go into answering or clarifying.

211
00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:19.000
And clarifying here is asking extra questions to the user in order to understand how deep it should research

212
00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:22.000
and how it should focus its deep research.

213
00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:28.000
And then after that is done, it goes into a crew function that actually does the entire research for you

214
00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:31.000
using the regular crew pattern that we have seen before,

215
00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:37.000
that then goes into a generateReport function at the end that triggers back into the answer.

216
00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:43.000
So in here you have a very easy to parse visual model of your entire code

217
00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:47.000
so that you understand what is happening and what each function is responsible for.

218
00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:53.000
And this is something that I'm so excited about because as an engineer myself make my life so much easier.

219
00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:59.000
So if you want to start with a flow on your own and create an entire folder structure,

220
00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:02.000
you actually have one simple command that you can use.

221
00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:05.000
And that is CrewAI createFlow.

222
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:09.000
All you got to do is type that into your terminal and give your flow a name

223
00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:14.000
and that will automatically create the entire project for you.

224
00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:17.000
And let's go over that folder structure just for one second.

225
00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:24.000
You can see in here that this is just a regular Python project using PyProject to define its versions and dependencies.

226
00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:30.000
And you can see that it's structured the host first directory to support your flow.

227
00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:36.000
So you're going to have one folder specific for crews, where in this case we have a research crew in there.

228
00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:42.000
And you have a specific folder for tools that can be reused across the entire crews.

229
00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:48.000
And your actually flow will leave in that main.py file where you can actually encode all your functions in there.

230
00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:51.000
And again, you can split this into different files if you want to.

231
00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:56.000
In the end of the day, flow is such a thin layer that it's all regular Python.

232
00:16:56.000 --> 00:17:05.000
So you can break things apart in any way that pleases you to make sure that this is something that is easily maintainable for you and for your team.

233
00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:12.000
Another very interesting thing about flows is that every flow has its own state and state management logic.

234
00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:17.000
And for the sake of comparison here, you can just think about this as being like a database.

235
00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:21.000
Where you can store anything during the flow execution in there.

236
00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:27.000
So let's say that from the start, from the first function, you produce some sort of data.

237
00:17:27.000 --> 00:17:33.000
Either by doing the LLM calls or by executing codes or maybe the final output of your crews or an agent.

238
00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:36.000
And you want to store that somewhere to be used later.

239
00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:43.000
You can just write that into your flow state and eventually in other functions you can read that out.

240
00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:52.000
So that allows you to have this one shared pocket of data that during a flow execution you can write things in and read things from.

241
00:17:52.000 --> 00:18:01.000
And that gives you so much flexibility on how you share all this data among the different functions as they get executed.

242
00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:06.000
But not only that, you can also persist that state.

243
00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:08.000
So that is stored in an actual database.

244
00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:17.000
So if you ever run that flow again, that state gets automatically preloaded from where it was saved and you can kick things off from where you started.

245
00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:25.000
And in order to do that, you can do something as simple as annotating the entire flow with a persist decorator.

246
00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:32.000
And that will automatically make sure that it's storing that into a local database for you when you're running this on your own computer.

247
00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:39.000
So you can think about how important this is because in our early example about the deep research conversation,

248
00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:52.000
as we send new messages, we want to make sure that we're persisting the entire messages and the outputs into this one single state that we can reuse as the conversation goes through.

249
00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:57.000
So there's an actual conversational history as the flow gets executed.

250
00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:06.000
So every time that we run the flow from the top, it automatically preloads all the conversations up to this point and allows you to just keep chatting with that.

251
00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:17.000
And again, that information can not only be used through all the functions, but it's also being used now across many flows executions because it's actually being persisted and preloaded.

252
00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:26.000
And a very cool thing is that as checkpoints, you can not only persist the entire state of the flow of the end of every single function,

253
00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:35.000
but you can be as granular as you want by going per function and defining on each function what are the functions that should actually persist the state.

254
00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:45.000
All you got to do is actually use this persist decorator to annotate every function that you want that by the end of that function to persist that state in the database as it is.

255
00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:53.000
And whatever one is the final version of that state, that is going to be automatically reloaded on your new execution of our flow.

256
00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:56.000
And you can still overwrite some of that if you want to.

257
00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:00.000
And we're going to see an example of that in a second once that we actually get our hands dirty.

258
00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:05.000
So I don't know about you, but flows feel so incredibly useful.

259
00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:09.000
And frankly, I'm seeing a lot of people using them on the day to day.

260
00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:14.000
And that has been their choosing way to move some of these use cases into a production environment.

261
00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:19.000
Because it allows you to really set this structure and have as many control as you want.

262
00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:28.000
And by applying this opt-in agency model, decide how much agency and how much intelligence you want to add into your automation.

263
00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:33.000
That said, they need to be designed well to work properly and they need to be built reliably.

264
00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:38.000
So in the next video, we're actually going to get our hands dirty and build this entire flow ourselves.

265
00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:42.000
I'm very excited about this. It's going to look awesome and you're going to love it.

266
00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:44.000
So let's jump right in there.
